Exact large integers in parallel runners
=======================================

Run ./maxima-local --no-init --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=["rtest_parallel"]);'
The appended cases are also part of the normal core and core/share suites.
They require neither GMP nor a Python installation. They exercise the Lisp
runtime already selected by Maxima; no arithmetic primitive is replaced.

The helper constructs 64 cases with 1..8192-bit operands, including limb
boundaries, all sign combinations, zero results and multiplication by zero
and one. Thirteen exact results cover sum, difference, product, gcd, floor,
modulus, integer square roots of a square and its neighbors, and cancellation.
References are constructed from binary identities rather than by repeating
serial multiplication. For example, (2^n-1)(2^n+1)=2^(2n)-1. A dividend's
reference quotient/remainder is constructed with shifts and additions, and
negative dividends check floor rather than truncation toward zero.

Both Common Lisp primitives and Maxima arithmetic helpers must match those
integer references exactly. Caller, worker, serial fallback, public scheduler
and nested runs are checked. Native probes synchronize two distinct workers
before arithmetic and keep both alive until their repeated checks finish.
They share the same input integer objects and verify unchanged decimal input
snapshots. A missing worker, early exit, changed input, non-integer result or
empty/truncated case set cannot satisfy the checks. Unsupported native thread
configurations still run the portable fallback cases. The probes join their
workers even if setup fails; waits are bounded.

GMP and the selected runtime
----------------------------

GMP's reentrancy contract permits simultaneous reads of an operand. Concurrent
read/write or write/write of the same mutable GMP object is not supported.
Global allocation hooks, default floating precision and random-state APIs
have separate restrictions. See https://gmplib.org/manual/Reentrancy .
Immutable Common Lisp integer values are distinct from foreign mutable mpz_t
objects. These tests do not exercise shared writable foreign objects or alter
GMP configuration, and passing them cannot certify every build or extension.

Record build_info(), the enabled module and loaded library before attributing
results to GMP. ECL's big-integer implementation uses GMP; its source maps
_ecl_big_mul to mpz_mul in src/h/number.h. The exact library still depends on
the installed ECL build. Default SBCL and SBCL with its optional SB-GMP module
are separate configurations. Requiring SB-GMP installs runtime dispatch
patches; large products use GMP while smaller operations can retain native
paths. The SBCL source documents that dispatch:
https://github.com/sbcl/sbcl/blob/sbcl-2.2.9/contrib/sb-gmp/gmp.lisp

For an optional SB-GMP comparison, start a fresh SBCL process, require :sb-gmp
on its own :lisp line, inspect SB-GMP:*GMP-VERSION* and *GMP-DISABLED*, then run
rtest_parallel. Reject any run reporting a Lisp load/error message; a suite
headline alone does not prove that the module loaded. Keep this comparison
separate from the default-runtime run. No optional module is enabled by the
standard tests, and a Lisp implementation's name alone does not establish its
GMP version or linkage.

The initial reference generator was independently checked against Python's
exact integer operations on four Lisps. Fault probes distinguish wrong signs,
word truncation, floor/truncate confusion, adjacent roots, approximate results,
input mutation, omitted cases and bypassed native execution. This is evidence
for these arithmetic paths, not an asynchronous-interrupt or GC-stress test.


An observed version-specific limitation
--------------------------------------

The optional SB-GMP shipped with SBCL 2.2.9 fails on complex rational powers;
Maxima exposes it in rtest_bernstein problem 11. It reproduces without these
new tests and without parallel execution. The upstream fix sends non-rational
bases back to the original exponentiation routine:
https://github.com/sbcl/sbcl/commit/c998d48297f773621fdbd6a6bb8939060c3eeeba

The integer checks pass in that configuration, while the full suite still
detects this separate loss of complex arithmetic. Validate the exact runtime
and module together; passing bignum checks does not replace the full suite.
